# FOSS Studio: what Docker needs, and nothing else.
#
# The domain, this server's public address, the login and the secrets are
# not here any more. They are settings inside the studio: it makes its
# own secrets on the first start, and the first time you open it in a
# browser it asks for the code it printed in its log and then for the
# password you want. Nothing in this file is secret, and a studio runs
# perfectly well with no .env at all.
#
# Copy to .env only if you want to change one of the defaults below.

# Port the studio answers on, published host:container with the same
# number on both sides (APP_BIND below decides who may reach it)
#HTTP_PORT=3000

# Where the data volume lives on this machine. Recordings, settings, the
# studio's own secrets and the relay's generated config are all in here.
#DATA_PATH=./data

# The ports published for media, one-to-one, host and container alike.
# They are few on purpose: mediasoup multiplexes every connection over
# them, so four carry a full room, and every published port costs Docker
# a proxy process. Move the range if several studios share a host.
#RTC_MIN_PORT=40000
#RTC_MAX_PORT=40003

# The same for the TURN relay's range. Thirty ports: a full room is
# twenty-four relayed connections at the very worst, and this leaves
# some over.
#TURN_MIN_PORT=49160
#TURN_MAX_PORT=49189

# Two different things decide who can reach the web port, and they are
# easy to confuse.
#
# BIND_HOST is the address the app listens on INSIDE its container. The
# image sets it to 0.0.0.0 and it should stay there: a published port
# with the app on loopback reaches nothing. (Run the app straight from
# Node with no container and it defaults to 127.0.0.1 instead.)
#BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0
#
# APP_BIND is the host side of the port mapping, and that is what really
# limits exposure. Left unset the web port answers anything that can
# route here, so a management panel can show you a link. Set it to
# 127.0.0.1 once a proxy on this machine is in front of the app, or to a
# Tailscale address for a tailnet-only studio.
#APP_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Optional: an old domain that should forward to your studio's domain,
# so old links keep working. This is the one thing that still wants
# DOMAIN here as well, because Caddy writes the redirect itself and
# cannot ask the studio where it lives.
#LEGACY_DOMAIN=old.example.com
#DOMAIN=studio.example.com

# Optional: folder of extra .caddy site files served by the bundled Caddy,
# for other apps sharing this host's ports 80/443 (leave unset if none)
#CADDY_SITES_PATH=/opt/caddy/sites

# Optional: folder of static web roots for those extra sites, mounted
# read-only at /srv/www inside Caddy - a site file can then serve files
# with "root * /srv/www/<name>". Point it OUTSIDE this app's folder
# (releases are replaced on every deploy), e.g. next to the sites path.
#CADDY_WWW_PATH=/opt/caddy/www

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read but no longer wanted. A studio that still has these keeps working
# exactly as it did and says so once in its log on every start. Clear
# them, restart, and set the same things in the studio's own Settings -
# then there is no secret in a file for anybody to copy or paste.
#
#DOMAIN=
#PUBLIC_IP=
#HOST_PASSWORD=
#SESSION_SECRET=
#TURN_SECRET=
#TURN_HOST=
#HOST_DOMAIN=
